(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("virtualization",{"virtualization/what-virtualization-is-and-hypervisor-types":{"html":"\u003cp>A server with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB of memory usually runs far below its limits when it hosts one application. Virtualization lets one physical machine, the host, run many virtual machines, called guests, each with its own operating system, and shares the hardware between them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a hypervisor does and why modern CPUs help it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Type 1 and type 2 hypervisors, with KVM, ESXi, Hyper-V and Proxmox as examples\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a virtual machine differs from a container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The pieces of a KVM host: kernel module, QEMU, libvirt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-hypervisor-does\">What a hypervisor does\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>hypervisor\u003c/strong> is the software that creates virtual machines and hands each one a share of CPU, memory, disk and network. A guest believes it owns a whole computer. The hypervisor keeps guests apart, so a crash or an attack inside one guest does not reach the others.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fast virtualization depends on \u003cstrong>hardware support\u003c/strong>. On Intel this is VT-x, on AMD it is AMD-V. These CPU features let guest code run directly on the processor, and only a small set of sensitive operations is trapped by the hypervisor. Without them, virtualization is slow or needs tricks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -E -o 'vmx|svm' /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u\nlsmod | grep kvm\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -E -o \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;vmx|svm&#x27;\u003c/span> /proc/cpuinfo | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -u\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vmx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsmod | grep kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kvm_intel             380928  0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kvm                  1142784  1 kvm_intel\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>vmx\u003c/code> means Intel VT-x is present and \u003ccode>svm\u003c/code> means AMD-V. If neither appears, the feature may be off in the firmware settings, or you are already inside a virtual machine that does not pass it through.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"type-1-and-type-2\">Type 1 and type 2\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Runs on\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Type 1 (bare metal)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Directly on hardware\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen, Proxmox VE\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Servers and private clouds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Type 2 (hosted)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inside a normal operating system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Laptops, testing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The line is not perfectly sharp. \u003cstrong>KVM\u003c/strong> is a part of the Linux kernel, so Linux itself becomes the hypervisor, which is why KVM is usually counted as type 1. \u003cstrong>Proxmox VE\u003c/strong> is a Debian-based system that bundles KVM virtual machines, containers, clustering and a web interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-kvm-stack\">The KVM stack\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>KVM\u003c/strong> is the kernel module that uses the CPU features.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>QEMU\u003c/strong> is the user-space program that emulates the rest of the machine: devices, disks, network cards, and it is the process you see for each guest.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>libvirt\u003c/strong> is the management layer. Tools such as \u003ccode>virsh\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>virt-manager\u003c/code> talk to libvirt, which starts QEMU with the right options.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>virtio\u003c/strong> is a family of paravirtual drivers. A guest with virtio disk and network drivers is much faster than one that uses emulated hardware.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh list --all\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh list --all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Id   Name        State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 3    web-01      running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> -    batch-02    shut off\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-machines-and-containers\">Virtual machines and containers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A virtual machine includes a full guest kernel. A container shares the host kernel and isolates processes with namespaces and cgroups. That makes containers lighter and faster to start, and virtual machines a stronger boundary because an attacker has to escape a hypervisor, not just a shared kernel. Many platforms run containers inside virtual machines to get both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-guest-that-would-not-start\">Incident: The guest that would not start\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new KVM host refused to start any guest and the team assumed the image was broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh start web-01\nlsmod | grep kvm\ngrep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh start web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: Failed to start domain 'web-01'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unsupported configuration: Domain requires KVM, but it is not available. Check that virtualization is enabled in the host BIOS, and host configuration is setup to load the kvm modules.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsmod | grep kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The image was fine. Hardware virtualization was switched off in the server firmware, so the CPU did not advertise \u003ccode>vmx\u003c/code> and the kvm module could not load. After the setting was enabled and the host restarted, the same command worked. When a guest will not start, check the host before the guest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Judging a host by CPU use alone and ignoring memory, which is usually the first limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running guests on emulated disk and network devices instead of virtio\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that nested virtualization (a hypervisor inside a guest) needs the setting enabled on the outer host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a virtual machine as a security boundary that needs no patching\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-hypervisor-does","text":"What a hypervisor does"},{"id":"type-1-and-type-2","text":"Type 1 and type 2"},{"id":"the-kvm-stack","text":"The KVM stack"},{"id":"virtual-machines-and-containers","text":"Virtual machines and containers"},{"id":"incident-the-guest-that-would-not-start","text":"Incident: The guest that would not start"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KVM documentation","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Documentation"},{"t":"libvirt documentation","u":"https://libvirt.org/docs.html"},{"t":"QEMU documentation","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/"}],"text":"A server with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB of memory usually runs far below its limits when it hosts one application. Virtualization lets one physical machine, the host, run many virtual machines, called guests, each with its own operating system, and shares the hardware between them. What you will learn What a hypervisor does and why modern CPUs help it Type 1 and type 2 hypervisors, with KVM, ESXi, Hyper-V and Proxmox as examples How a virtual machine differs from a container The pieces of a KVM host: kernel module, QEMU, libvirt What a hypervisor does A hypervisor is the software that creates virtual machines and hands each one a share of CPU, memory, disk and network. A guest believes it owns a whole computer. The hypervisor keeps guests apart, so a crash or an attack inside one guest does not reach the others. Fast virtualization depends on hardware support . On Intel this is VT-x, on AMD it is AMD-V. These CPU features let guest code run directly on the processor, and only a small set of sensitive operations is trapped by the hypervisor. Without them, virtualization is slow or needs tricks. $ grep -E -o &#x27;vmx|svm&#x27; /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u vmx $ lsmod | grep kvm kvm_intel 380928 0 kvm 1142784 1 kvm_intel vmx means Intel VT-x is present and svm means AMD-V. If neither appears, the feature may be off in the firmware settings, or you are already inside a virtual machine that does not pass it through. Type 1 and type 2 Kind Runs on Examples Typical use Type 1 (bare metal) Directly on hardware VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen, Proxmox VE Servers and private clouds Type 2 (hosted) Inside a normal operating system VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels Laptops, testing The line is not perfectly sharp. KVM is a part of the Linux kernel, so Linux itself becomes the hypervisor, which is why KVM is usually counted as type 1. Proxmox VE is a Debian-based system that bundles KVM virtual machines, containers, clustering and a web interface. The KVM stack KVM is the kernel module that uses the CPU features. QEMU is the user-space program that emulates the rest of the machine: devices, disks, network cards, and it is the process you see for each guest. libvirt is the management layer. Tools such as virsh and virt-manager talk to libvirt, which starts QEMU with the right options. virtio is a family of paravirtual drivers. A guest with virtio disk and network drivers is much faster than one that uses emulated hardware. $ virsh list --all Id Name State ------------------------------ 3 web-01 running - batch-02 shut off Virtual machines and containers A virtual machine includes a full guest kernel. A container shares the host kernel and isolates processes with namespaces and cgroups. That makes containers lighter and faster to start, and virtual machines a stronger boundary because an attacker has to escape a hypervisor, not just a shared kernel. Many platforms run containers inside virtual machines to get both. Incident: The guest that would not start Incident A new KVM host refused to start any guest and the team assumed the image was broken. $ virsh start web-01 error: Failed to start domain 'web-01' error: unsupported configuration: Domain requires KVM, but it is not available. Check that virtualization is enabled in the host BIOS, and host configuration is setup to load the kvm modules. $ lsmod | grep kvm $ grep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo 0 The image was fine. Hardware virtualization was switched off in the server firmware, so the CPU did not advertise vmx and the kvm module could not load. After the setting was enabled and the host restarted, the same command worked. When a guest will not start, check the host before the guest. Common mistakes Judging a host by CPU use alone and ignoring memory, which is usually the first limit Running guests on emulated disk and network devices instead of virtio Forgetting that nested virtualization (a hypervisor inside a guest) needs the setting enabled on the outer host Treating a virtual ma"},"virtualization/cpu-memory-and-overcommit":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud and private cloud hosts rarely give every guest dedicated hardware. They promise more virtual CPUs and memory than exist, and rely on guests not asking for it all at once. That is \u003cstrong>overcommit\u003c/strong>. It saves money until the day it does not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How virtual CPUs are scheduled onto physical cores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory overcommit, ballooning, KSM and swapping\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU ready time and steal time, the signs of a crowded host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NUMA and why a large guest can run slower than a small one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-cpus\">Virtual CPUs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>vCPU\u003c/strong> is a thread of the host that runs a guest&#39;s CPU. If a host has 16 cores (32 with hyper-threading) and the guests together have 80 vCPUs, the ratio is 2.5 vCPUs per thread. Light workloads often tolerate 4 to 1 or more, busy production databases need close to 1 to 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When more vCPUs want to run than there are free threads, some wait. The guest sees this as \u003cstrong>steal time\u003c/strong>, shown as \u003ccode>st\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> inside a Linux guest. VMware shows the same idea as \u003cstrong>CPU ready\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"top -bn1 | head -4 | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>top -bn1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">%Cpu(s): 22.0 us,  6.0 sy,  0.0 ni, 54.0 id,  0.0 wa,  0.0 hi,  0.0 si, 18.0 st\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Eighteen percent steal means the guest wanted CPU and the host could not give it. The guest looks idle at 54 percent, yet it is slow. The fix is on the host: move guests away, reduce vCPU counts, or add hardware.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory\">Memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Memory is the harder resource, because a guest that is out of memory fails instead of slowing down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ballooning\u003c/strong> lets the host ask a guest driver to take memory back. The guest swaps or drops cache to comply.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>KSM (kernel samepage merging)\u003c/strong> shares identical pages between guests. It saves memory and costs some CPU.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Host swap\u003c/strong> is the last resort. A guest whose memory is swapped by the host becomes very slow, because the guest does not know.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Huge pages\u003c/strong> reduce overhead for large guests such as databases.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"free -m\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>free -m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">               total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Mem:          262144      249910        1210         312       11024        9800\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Swap:          32768       14200       18568\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When the host itself starts using swap with guests running, memory is overcommitted too far. A common rule is to keep memory overcommit near 1 to 1 for production, and to allow more only where guests are idle and can be paused.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numa\">NUMA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Large servers have several CPU sockets, each with memory attached. Reading memory from another socket is slower. A guest bigger than one socket may span both and lose speed. Where possible, pin large guests to one NUMA node, or size them to fit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-monday-morning-slowdown\">Incident: The Monday morning slowdown\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every Monday at 09:00 the web guests slowed, yet their graphs showed low CPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">guest$ top -bn1 | grep Cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">%Cpu(s): 15.0 us,  4.0 sy,  0.0 ni, 40.0 id,  0.0 wa,  0.0 hi,  0.0 si, 41.0 st\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ virsh nodeinfo | grep -E 'CPU\\(s\\)|Memory'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CPU(s):              32\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Memory size:         268435456 KiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ virsh list | wc -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">39\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Forty-one percent steal showed the guests were waiting for the host. Thirty-six running guests shared 32 threads, and a weekly batch of reports started at 09:00 on the same host. The team moved the batch guests to a separate host and set a limit of 3 vCPUs per thread for that cluster. Guest graphs inside the guest can hide this problem. Steal time and host CPU tell the truth.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Give every VM 16 vCPUs so none of them is ever short of CPU.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># 20 guests, each with &lt;vcpu&gt;16&lt;/vcpu&gt; on a 32-core host\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ top\n%Cpu(s): ... 31.4 st\n# guests report high load and slow responses\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Many vCPUs make the scheduler find many free cores at once, so a guest with 16 vCPUs waits longer and steal time rises for everybody. More vCPUs is not more speed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># start small and measure\n&lt;vcpu&gt;2&lt;/vcpu&gt;\n# watch steal time\nvmstat 5 3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Size guests to their real needs, increase only when the guest is CPU bound, and watch steal time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving every guest many vCPUs &quot;to be safe&quot;: more vCPUs can make scheduling worse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommitting memory on a host that runs databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only guest CPU graphs and ignoring steal or ready time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting a guest larger than a NUMA node without pinning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"virtual-cpus","text":"Virtual CPUs"},{"id":"memory","text":"Memory"},{"id":"numa","text":"NUMA"},{"id":"incident-the-monday-morning-slowdown","text":"Incident: The Monday morning slowdown"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KVM: Documentation","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Documentation"},{"t":"Linux kernel: KSM","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/ksm.html"},{"t":"libvirt: NUMA tuning","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatdomain.html#numa-node-tuning"}],"text":"Cloud and private cloud hosts rarely give every guest dedicated hardware. They promise more virtual CPUs and memory than exist, and rely on guests not asking for it all at once. That is overcommit . It saves money until the day it does not. What you will learn How virtual CPUs are scheduled onto physical cores Memory overcommit, ballooning, KSM and swapping CPU ready time and steal time, the signs of a crowded host NUMA and why a large guest can run slower than a small one Virtual CPUs A vCPU is a thread of the host that runs a guest&#39;s CPU. If a host has 16 cores (32 with hyper-threading) and the guests together have 80 vCPUs, the ratio is 2.5 vCPUs per thread. Light workloads often tolerate 4 to 1 or more, busy production databases need close to 1 to 1. When more vCPUs want to run than there are free threads, some wait. The guest sees this as steal time , shown as st in top inside a Linux guest. VMware shows the same idea as CPU ready . $ top -bn1 | head -4 | tail -2 %Cpu(s): 22.0 us, 6.0 sy, 0.0 ni, 54.0 id, 0.0 wa, 0.0 hi, 0.0 si, 18.0 st Eighteen percent steal means the guest wanted CPU and the host could not give it. The guest looks idle at 54 percent, yet it is slow. The fix is on the host: move guests away, reduce vCPU counts, or add hardware. Memory Memory is the harder resource, because a guest that is out of memory fails instead of slowing down. Ballooning lets the host ask a guest driver to take memory back. The guest swaps or drops cache to comply. KSM (kernel samepage merging) shares identical pages between guests. It saves memory and costs some CPU. Host swap is the last resort. A guest whose memory is swapped by the host becomes very slow, because the guest does not know. Huge pages reduce overhead for large guests such as databases. $ free -m total used free shared buff/cache available Mem: 262144 249910 1210 312 11024 9800 Swap: 32768 14200 18568 When the host itself starts using swap with guests running, memory is overcommitted too far. A common rule is to keep memory overcommit near 1 to 1 for production, and to allow more only where guests are idle and can be paused. NUMA Large servers have several CPU sockets, each with memory attached. Reading memory from another socket is slower. A guest bigger than one socket may span both and lose speed. Where possible, pin large guests to one NUMA node, or size them to fit. Incident: The Monday morning slowdown Incident Every Monday at 09:00 the web guests slowed, yet their graphs showed low CPU. guest$ top -bn1 | grep Cpu %Cpu(s): 15.0 us, 4.0 sy, 0.0 ni, 40.0 id, 0.0 wa, 0.0 hi, 0.0 si, 41.0 st host$ virsh nodeinfo | grep -E 'CPU\\(s\\)|Memory' CPU(s): 32 Memory size: 268435456 KiB host$ virsh list | wc -l 39 Forty-one percent steal showed the guests were waiting for the host. Thirty-six running guests shared 32 threads, and a weekly batch of reports started at 09:00 on the same host. The team moved the batch guests to a separate host and set a limit of 3 vCPUs per thread for that cluster. Guest graphs inside the guest can hide this problem. Steal time and host CPU tell the truth. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Give every VM 16 vCPUs so none of them is ever short of CPU. The tempting way # 20 guests, each with \u003cvcpu>16\u003c/vcpu> on a 32-core host See what goes wrong $ top %Cpu(s): ... 31.4 st # guests report high load and slow responses Why it fails. Many vCPUs make the scheduler find many free cores at once, so a guest with 16 vCPUs waits longer and steal time rises for everybody. More vCPUs is not more speed. See the way that works The way that works # start small and measure \u003cvcpu>2\u003c/vcpu> # watch steal time vmstat 5 3 Size guests to their real needs, increase only when the guest is CPU bound, and watch steal time. Common mistakes Giving every guest many vCPUs \"to be safe\": more vCPUs can make scheduling worse Overcommitting memory on a host that runs databases Reading only guest CPU graphs and ignoring steal or ready time Starting a guest larger than"},"virtualization/virtual-disks-snapshots-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>A virtual machine&#39;s disk is a file or a volume on the host. How that file is stored decides speed, space use and what happens when you take a snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Common image formats: raw, qcow2, VMDK, VHDX\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Thin and thick provisioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a snapshot works and why long chains cause trouble\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Templates, clones and cloud-init\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"image-formats\">Image formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Format\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Used by\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>raw\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>KVM, many clouds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple and fast, no features of its own\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>qcow2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>KVM and QEMU\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thin provisioning, snapshots, compression\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>VMDK\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VMware\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thin or thick, snapshots as delta files\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>VHDX\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hyper-V\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Dynamic or fixed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"qemu-img info web-01.qcow2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>qemu-img info web-01.qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">image: web-01.qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">file format: qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">virtual size: 100 GiB (107374182400 bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">disk size: 8.2 GiB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>virtual size\u003c/strong> is what the guest sees. The \u003cstrong>disk size\u003c/strong> is what the file takes on the host. That gap is \u003cstrong>thin provisioning\u003c/strong>: space is taken only as the guest writes. It saves space and lets you promise more than you have, which is another kind of overcommit. If every guest fills its disk, the storage pool runs out and guests fail with write errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots\">Snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A snapshot freezes the disk at a point in time. New writes go to a separate file, called a delta or overlay, and the original becomes read-only. Rolling back means discarding the delta.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade\nvirsh snapshot-list web-01\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Domain snapshot before-upgrade created\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Name             Creation Time               State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> before-upgrade   2026-11-02 10:14:09 +0000   shutoff\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A snapshot is not a backup. It lives on the same storage as the disk, so losing the storage loses both. Each snapshot adds a layer: reads may have to look through several files, which slows the guest, and every delta uses space. A \u003cstrong>chain\u003c/strong> of dozens of snapshots can fill a pool and make deleting them slow because deltas must be merged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Use snapshots for short-lived safety, such as before an upgrade, and delete them when the change is accepted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-and-clones\">Templates and clones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>template\u003c/strong> is a prepared guest image with the operating system installed and cleaned. A \u003cstrong>full clone\u003c/strong> copies the disk. A \u003cstrong>linked clone\u003c/strong> uses the template as a read-only base and stores only the differences, so it is fast and small, but depends on the base.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>cloud-init\u003c/strong> configures a guest at first boot from user data: host name, users, SSH keys, packages. It lets one generic template serve many roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pool-that-filled-overnight\">Incident: The pool that filled overnight\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pool that was 60 percent full on Friday reached 100 percent on Sunday and every guest paused.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh pool-info vmstore\nvirsh snapshot-list db-01 | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh pool-info vmstore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Capacity:       500.00 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Allocation:     499.98 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Available:      0.02 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list db-01 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">42\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A backup tool created a snapshot of \u003ccode>db-01\u003c/code> every hour and never removed them. Forty snapshots each held the changes of an hour of database writes. Deleting the old snapshots merged the files and freed 180 GiB, and the guests resumed. The team added a retention rule and an alert at 80 percent pool use.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Keep a snapshot of the database VM for safety, forever.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>virsh snapshot-create-as db-1 before-upgrade\n# ... six months later, still there\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ virsh pool-info default\nAvailable: 3.2 GiB\nerror: Write error on db-1: No space left on device\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A snapshot is not a backup. Every write after it lands in a growing overlay, reads get slower along the chain and the pool eventually fills, which pauses every guest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>virsh snapshot-list db-1\nvirsh snapshot-delete db-1 before-upgrade\n# use real backups for protection\nvirsh domblklist db-1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Delete snapshots when the change is verified, and take backups with a tool built for it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating snapshots as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving snapshots for weeks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommitting thin disks with no alert on pool free space\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloning a guest without changing its host name, machine id and SSH host keys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"image-formats","text":"Image formats"},{"id":"snapshots","text":"Snapshots"},{"id":"templates-and-clones","text":"Templates and clones"},{"id":"incident-the-pool-that-filled-overnight","text":"Incident: The pool that filled overnight"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"QEMU: disk images","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/images.html"},{"t":"libvirt: snapshots","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatsnapshot.html"},{"t":"cloud-init documentation","u":"https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/"}],"text":"A virtual machine&#39;s disk is a file or a volume on the host. How that file is stored decides speed, space use and what happens when you take a snapshot. What you will learn Common image formats: raw, qcow2, VMDK, VHDX Thin and thick provisioning How a snapshot works and why long chains cause trouble Templates, clones and cloud-init Image formats Format Used by Notes raw KVM, many clouds Simple and fast, no features of its own qcow2 KVM and QEMU Thin provisioning, snapshots, compression VMDK VMware Thin or thick, snapshots as delta files VHDX Hyper-V Dynamic or fixed $ qemu-img info web-01.qcow2 image: web-01.qcow2 file format: qcow2 virtual size: 100 GiB (107374182400 bytes) disk size: 8.2 GiB The virtual size is what the guest sees. The disk size is what the file takes on the host. That gap is thin provisioning : space is taken only as the guest writes. It saves space and lets you promise more than you have, which is another kind of overcommit. If every guest fills its disk, the storage pool runs out and guests fail with write errors. Snapshots A snapshot freezes the disk at a point in time. New writes go to a separate file, called a delta or overlay, and the original becomes read-only. Rolling back means discarding the delta. $ virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade Domain snapshot before-upgrade created $ virsh snapshot-list web-01 Name Creation Time State ------------------------------------------------------ before-upgrade 2026-11-02 10:14:09 +0000 shutoff A snapshot is not a backup. It lives on the same storage as the disk, so losing the storage loses both. Each snapshot adds a layer: reads may have to look through several files, which slows the guest, and every delta uses space. A chain of dozens of snapshots can fill a pool and make deleting them slow because deltas must be merged. Use snapshots for short-lived safety, such as before an upgrade, and delete them when the change is accepted. Templates and clones A template is a prepared guest image with the operating system installed and cleaned. A full clone copies the disk. A linked clone uses the template as a read-only base and stores only the differences, so it is fast and small, but depends on the base. cloud-init configures a guest at first boot from user data: host name, users, SSH keys, packages. It lets one generic template serve many roles. Incident: The pool that filled overnight Incident A pool that was 60 percent full on Friday reached 100 percent on Sunday and every guest paused. $ virsh pool-info vmstore Capacity: 500.00 GiB Allocation: 499.98 GiB Available: 0.02 GiB $ virsh snapshot-list db-01 | wc -l 42 A backup tool created a snapshot of db-01 every hour and never removed them. Forty snapshots each held the changes of an hour of database writes. Deleting the old snapshots merged the files and freed 180 GiB, and the guests resumed. The team added a retention rule and an alert at 80 percent pool use. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Keep a snapshot of the database VM for safety, forever. The tempting way virsh snapshot-create-as db-1 before-upgrade # ... six months later, still there See what goes wrong $ virsh pool-info default Available: 3.2 GiB error: Write error on db-1: No space left on device Why it fails. A snapshot is not a backup. Every write after it lands in a growing overlay, reads get slower along the chain and the pool eventually fills, which pauses every guest. See the way that works The way that works virsh snapshot-list db-1 virsh snapshot-delete db-1 before-upgrade # use real backups for protection virsh domblklist db-1 Delete snapshots when the change is verified, and take backups with a tool built for it. Common mistakes Treating snapshots as backups Leaving snapshots for weeks Overcommitting thin disks with no alert on pool free space Cloning a guest without changing its host name, machine id and SSH host keys"},"virtualization/virtual-networking-bridges-and-vlans":{"html":"\u003cp>A guest needs a network card, and the card needs somewhere to plug in. The hypervisor provides a virtual switch for that, and the choices you make there decide who can reach the guest and what it can reach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Virtual NICs and the virtual switch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bridged, NAT and isolated networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VLAN tagging on the host uplink\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SR-IOV and when to skip the virtual switch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-virtual-switch\">The virtual switch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each guest has one or more \u003cstrong>virtual NICs\u003c/strong>, usually virtio. On the host they appear as interfaces such as \u003ccode>vnet3\u003c/code> attached to a \u003cstrong>bridge\u003c/strong> or a vSwitch. On Linux this is often the \u003ccode>br0\u003c/code> bridge or an Open vSwitch bridge. VMware calls it a vSwitch, and Hyper-V a virtual switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ip -br link show master br0\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip -br \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">link\u003c/span> show master br0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1      UP    3c:ec:ef:11:22:33\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet0     UP    fe:54:00:aa:bb:01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet1     UP    fe:54:00:aa:bb:02\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The physical port \u003ccode>eno1\u003c/code> and the guest ports \u003ccode>vnet0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vnet1\u003c/code> are on one bridge, so the guests behave like machines plugged into the same switch as the host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-modes\">Network modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Bridged\u003c/strong>: guests have addresses on the physical network and are visible to it. Common for servers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NAT\u003c/strong>: the host hides guests behind its own address. Guests can go out, but nothing can reach them unless you forward ports. This is libvirt&#39;s default \u003ccode>virbr0\u003c/code> network, convenient for desktops and tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Isolated\u003c/strong>: guests can reach each other and the host but not the outside.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Routed\u003c/strong>: the host routes between a guest network and the physical one without translation.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vlans\">VLANs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>VLAN\u003c/strong> splits one physical network into separate broadcast domains using a tag in the Ethernet frame. The switch port to the host is often a \u003cstrong>trunk\u003c/strong> that carries several VLANs, and the host or hypervisor assigns each guest to one. If the trunk does not allow the tag, or the guest is on the wrong tag, the guest has a link but no connectivity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bridge vlan show\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bridge vlan show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1    1 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet0   20 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet1   30 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sr-iov\">SR-IOV\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>SR-IOV\u003c/strong> lets one physical NIC present several virtual functions that are handed directly to guests. It skips the software switch, so latency and CPU use drop. The cost is flexibility: live migration and firewall rules at the host become harder.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-new-guest-with-a-link-and-no-network\">Incident: A new guest with a link and no network\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A guest came up, got no address from DHCP, and the team blamed the DHCP server.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">guest$ ip -br addr\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ens3   UP   fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456/64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ bridge vlan show dev vnet5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet5   40 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ bridge vlan show dev eno1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1    1 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        30\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The guest was assigned to VLAN 40, but the uplink \u003ccode>eno1\u003c/code> only carried VLANs 20 and 30. The DHCP server was fine. After the network team added VLAN 40 to the trunk, the guest received an address. Check each hop of the path: guest, vnet port, bridge, uplink, switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting a guest on a NAT network and then wondering why clients cannot reach it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a trunk port must allow every VLAN used\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Two guests with the same MAC address after a clone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling the firewall on the host because a guest could not connect, without checking the VLAN first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-virtual-switch","text":"The virtual switch"},{"id":"network-modes","text":"Network modes"},{"id":"vlans","text":"VLANs"},{"id":"sr-iov","text":"SR-IOV"},{"id":"incident-a-new-guest-with-a-link-and-no-network","text":"Incident: A new guest with a link and no network"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt: networking","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatnetwork.html"},{"t":"Linux kernel: bridge","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/networking/bridge.html"},{"t":"Open vSwitch documentation","u":"https://docs.openvswitch.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"A guest needs a network card, and the card needs somewhere to plug in. The hypervisor provides a virtual switch for that, and the choices you make there decide who can reach the guest and what it can reach. What you will learn Virtual NICs and the virtual switch Bridged, NAT and isolated networks VLAN tagging on the host uplink SR-IOV and when to skip the virtual switch The virtual switch Each guest has one or more virtual NICs , usually virtio. On the host they appear as interfaces such as vnet3 attached to a bridge or a vSwitch. On Linux this is often the br0 bridge or an Open vSwitch bridge. VMware calls it a vSwitch, and Hyper-V a virtual switch. $ ip -br link show master br0 eno1 UP 3c:ec:ef:11:22:33 vnet0 UP fe:54:00:aa:bb:01 vnet1 UP fe:54:00:aa:bb:02 The physical port eno1 and the guest ports vnet0 and vnet1 are on one bridge, so the guests behave like machines plugged into the same switch as the host. Network modes Bridged : guests have addresses on the physical network and are visible to it. Common for servers. NAT : the host hides guests behind its own address. Guests can go out, but nothing can reach them unless you forward ports. This is libvirt&#39;s default virbr0 network, convenient for desktops and tests. Isolated : guests can reach each other and the host but not the outside. Routed : the host routes between a guest network and the physical one without translation. VLANs A VLAN splits one physical network into separate broadcast domains using a tag in the Ethernet frame. The switch port to the host is often a trunk that carries several VLANs, and the host or hypervisor assigns each guest to one. If the trunk does not allow the tag, or the guest is on the wrong tag, the guest has a link but no connectivity. $ bridge vlan show port vlan-id eno1 1 PVID Egress Untagged 20 30 vnet0 20 PVID Egress Untagged vnet1 30 PVID Egress Untagged SR-IOV SR-IOV lets one physical NIC present several virtual functions that are handed directly to guests. It skips the software switch, so latency and CPU use drop. The cost is flexibility: live migration and firewall rules at the host become harder. Incident: A new guest with a link and no network Incident A guest came up, got no address from DHCP, and the team blamed the DHCP server. guest$ ip -br addr ens3 UP fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456/64 host$ bridge vlan show dev vnet5 port vlan-id vnet5 40 PVID Egress Untagged host$ bridge vlan show dev eno1 port vlan-id eno1 1 PVID Egress Untagged 20 30 The guest was assigned to VLAN 40, but the uplink eno1 only carried VLANs 20 and 30. The DHCP server was fine. After the network team added VLAN 40 to the trunk, the guest received an address. Check each hop of the path: guest, vnet port, bridge, uplink, switch. Common mistakes Putting a guest on a NAT network and then wondering why clients cannot reach it Forgetting that a trunk port must allow every VLAN used Two guests with the same MAC address after a clone Disabling the firewall on the host because a guest could not connect, without checking the VLAN first"},"virtualization/live-migration-ha-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>A host needs patching, or fails. The point of a virtual platform is that guests do not have to suffer for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Live migration and what it needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>High availability, fencing and split brain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity planning for failure (N+1)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist for the faults you will meet\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"live-migration\">Live migration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Live migration\u003c/strong> (VMware vMotion, KVM live migration) copies a running guest&#39;s memory from one host to another while it keeps running, then switches over in a short pause of tens of milliseconds. It makes host maintenance invisible to users.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Requirements usually include:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A shared or replicated storage that both hosts can reach, or storage migration that copies the disk too\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compatible CPU features: a guest that uses a new instruction cannot move to an older CPU, so clusters set a common \u003cstrong>CPU model\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fast network between hosts for the memory copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Matching networks on both hosts, so the guest keeps its connectivity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\nvirsh list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Id   Name   State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------------\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A guest that dirties memory faster than the network can copy it may never converge. Throttling the guest, or using post-copy migration, solves that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"high-availability\">High availability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>HA\u003c/strong> restarts guests on another host after a host failure. It differs from migration: the guest does restart, with a short outage. The cluster must be sure the failed host is really off before it starts the same guest elsewhere, otherwise two copies write to one disk and corrupt it. That is the danger of \u003cstrong>split brain\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Fencing\u003c/strong> (also called STONITH) cuts power or access to a suspect host to make sure it is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A cluster also needs \u003cstrong>quorum\u003c/strong>: a majority of nodes must agree on who is alive. With two nodes and no tie-breaker, a network split leaves each side unsure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-for-failure\">Plan for failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spare capacity must exist for the guests that restart. \u003cstrong>N+1\u003c/strong> means the cluster can lose one host and still run everything. If every host runs at 85 percent memory, a failed host leaves guests with nowhere to go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"troubleshooting-checklist\">Troubleshooting checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is hardware virtualization on, and is \u003ccode>kvm\u003c/code> loaded?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there free memory on the host for the guest, with overcommit counted?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there free space in the storage pool, including snapshot deltas?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the disk file present, with the right permissions and SELinux or AppArmor labels?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the guest on the right network and VLAN?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Are guests waiting for CPU (steal or ready time)?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -u libvirtd --since &quot;10 min ago&quot; | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u libvirtd --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10 min ago&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libvirtd[1043]: internal error: qemu unexpectedly closed the monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libvirtd[1043]: cannot set up guest memory 'pc.ram': Cannot allocate memory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-maintenance-that-moved-the-problem\">Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Draining a host for patching started a chain of guest failures on the next host.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory\nvirsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | wc -l\nvirsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Memory size:         134217728 KiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">19\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: internal error: process exited while connecting to monitor: Cannot allocate memory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Host B already used almost all of its memory. The migration failed, and then the HA service on host B restarted two guests during the same minute and exhausted memory. The team set a rule that a host may hold at most 80 percent of its memory with guests, so one host can always be drained into the rest, and drained one host at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running clusters at high memory use and calling it efficient\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing CPU generations without a common CPU model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HA with no fencing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a snapshot as the way to move a guest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"live-migration","text":"Live migration"},{"id":"high-availability","text":"High availability"},{"id":"plan-for-failure","text":"Plan for failure"},{"id":"troubleshooting-checklist","text":"Troubleshooting checklist"},{"id":"incident-maintenance-that-moved-the-problem","text":"Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt: migration","u":"https://libvirt.org/migration.html"},{"t":"QEMU: live migration","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/devel/migration/main.html"},{"t":"Proxmox VE documentation","u":"https://pve.proxmox.com/pve-docs/"}],"text":"A host needs patching, or fails. The point of a virtual platform is that guests do not have to suffer for it. What you will learn Live migration and what it needs High availability, fencing and split brain Capacity planning for failure (N+1) A checklist for the faults you will meet Live migration Live migration (VMware vMotion, KVM live migration) copies a running guest&#39;s memory from one host to another while it keeps running, then switches over in a short pause of tens of milliseconds. It makes host maintenance invisible to users. Requirements usually include: A shared or replicated storage that both hosts can reach, or storage migration that copies the disk too Compatible CPU features: a guest that uses a new instruction cannot move to an older CPU, so clusters set a common CPU model A fast network between hosts for the memory copy Matching networks on both hosts, so the guest keeps its connectivity $ virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system $ virsh list Id Name State ------------------- A guest that dirties memory faster than the network can copy it may never converge. Throttling the guest, or using post-copy migration, solves that. High availability HA restarts guests on another host after a host failure. It differs from migration: the guest does restart, with a short outage. The cluster must be sure the failed host is really off before it starts the same guest elsewhere, otherwise two copies write to one disk and corrupt it. That is the danger of split brain . Fencing (also called STONITH) cuts power or access to a suspect host to make sure it is down. A cluster also needs quorum : a majority of nodes must agree on who is alive. With two nodes and no tie-breaker, a network split leaves each side unsure. Plan for failure Spare capacity must exist for the guests that restart. N+1 means the cluster can lose one host and still run everything. If every host runs at 85 percent memory, a failed host leaves guests with nowhere to go. Troubleshooting checklist Is hardware virtualization on, and is kvm loaded? Is there free memory on the host for the guest, with overcommit counted? Is there free space in the storage pool, including snapshot deltas? Is the disk file present, with the right permissions and SELinux or AppArmor labels? Is the guest on the right network and VLAN? Are guests waiting for CPU (steal or ready time)? $ journalctl -u libvirtd --since \"10 min ago\" | tail -3 libvirtd[1043]: internal error: qemu unexpectedly closed the monitor libvirtd[1043]: cannot set up guest memory 'pc.ram': Cannot allocate memory Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem Incident Draining a host for patching started a chain of guest failures on the next host. $ virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory Memory size: 134217728 KiB $ virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | wc -l 19 $ virsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system error: internal error: process exited while connecting to monitor: Cannot allocate memory Host B already used almost all of its memory. The migration failed, and then the HA service on host B restarted two guests during the same minute and exhausted memory. The team set a rule that a host may hold at most 80 percent of its memory with guests, so one host can always be drained into the rest, and drained one host at a time. Common mistakes Running clusters at high memory use and calling it efficient Mixing CPU generations without a common CPU model HA with no fencing Using a snapshot as the way to move a guest"},"virtualization/virtualization-ops-capacity-overcommit-and-noisy-neighbours":{"html":"\u003cp>A hypervisor host shares CPU, memory, disk and network among guests. Overcommit is what makes virtualization economical, and contention is its price. Operations means choosing ratios with evidence, watching the right signals and moving guests before users feel pain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>CPU, memory and storage overcommit and safe starting ratios\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signals of contention: steal, ready time, ballooning, swapping and I/O wait\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NUMA, pinning and huge pages for demanding guests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placement and limits that contain noisy neighbours\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"overcommit-by-resource\">Overcommit by resource\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CPU\u003c/strong> can be overcommitted well, because most guests are idle most of the time. Common starting points are 4:1 virtual to physical cores for general workloads, lower (1:1 or 2:1) for latency-sensitive or heavy compute guests. Measure the real contention, not the ratio.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Memory\u003c/strong> is risky to overcommit. When the host runs out, it swaps or kills guests. Use modest ratios (often 1.0 to 1.5), enable ballooning and page sharing only with monitoring, and keep a reserve for the host.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Storage\u003c/strong> can be thin-provisioned, but the pool must be watched, since a full pool freezes many guests at once.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Treat these as starting points to tune with data, because the right values depend on your workloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detect-contention\">Detect contention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inside the guest: high \u003cstrong>steal time\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>%st\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>vmstat\u003c/code>) means the guest wanted CPU and the host did not give it. On the host:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh nodecpustats\nvirsh domstats --cpu-total --balloon --block --vcpu vm-web-01\nvirsh domblkstat vm-web-01 vda\nvirsh domifstat vm-web-01 vnet3\nnumastat -m | head -20\ncat /proc/pressure/cpu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh nodecpustats\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domstats --cpu-total --balloon --block --vcpu vm-web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domblkstat vm-web-01 vda\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domifstat vm-web-01 vnet3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>numastat -m | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/pressure/cpu\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pressure stall information in \u003ccode>/proc/pressure/\u003c/code> shows how much time tasks wait for CPU, memory or I/O on the host. A host whose CPU pressure rises while guest steal rises is oversubscribed. Memory pressure plus swap use on the host is an emergency, because every guest suffers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numa-pinning-and-huge-pages\">NUMA, pinning and huge pages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Large hosts have several NUMA nodes, each with local memory. A guest whose vCPUs run on one node but whose memory sits on another pays a latency cost. For performance-sensitive guests, keep vCPUs and memory on the same node (NUMA-aware placement), pin vCPUs to dedicated cores, and use huge pages to reduce page table overhead. These guests give up flexibility for predictability, so reserve them a dedicated host pool and do not overcommit it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contain-noisy-neighbours\">Contain noisy neighbours\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set limits and shares so a single guest cannot take everything: CPU shares and quotas, disk I/O throttling (IOPS and bandwidth) and network rate limits through libvirt or the cloud layer&#39;s flavors. Separate workload classes by host aggregate: a general pool with overcommit, a premium pool without it, a storage-heavy pool. Keep headroom to evacuate the largest host (N+1) and move guests early using live migration when a host trends toward contention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A guest shows 25 percent steal while its own processes are idle. What does it mean?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The hypervisor is not giving the guest the CPU it asks for: the host is oversubscribed. Move guests away, lower the ratio or reserve capacity.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Steal is lost CPU time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-monday-morning-stalls\">Incident: The Monday morning stalls\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every Monday at 09:00 several unrelated application servers on the same host became slow for about 20 minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh domstats --cpu-total vm-batch-07 | grep cpu.time\ncat /proc/pressure/cpu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domstats --cpu-total vm-batch-07 | grep cpu.time\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cpu.time=48230193847211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/pressure/cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">some avg10=62.40 avg60=58.12 avg300=40.77 total=19238421831\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A batch guest, scheduled to run weekly reports at 09:00, had 16 vCPUs and used all of them, while the host had 40 cores for 160 vCPUs of other guests. The other guests saw steal times above 20 percent. The team moved the batch guest to a dedicated aggregate with its own cores, set CPU shares, and added an alert on host CPU pressure and guest steal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overcommitting memory like CPU\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Judging by the ratio instead of measured contention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing latency-sensitive and batch guests on one pool\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No spare capacity to evacuate a failed host\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"overcommit-by-resource","text":"Overcommit by resource"},{"id":"detect-contention","text":"Detect contention"},{"id":"numa-pinning-and-huge-pages","text":"NUMA, pinning and huge pages"},{"id":"contain-noisy-neighbours","text":"Contain noisy neighbours"},{"id":"incident-the-monday-morning-stalls","text":"Incident: The Monday morning stalls"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt documentation","u":"https://libvirt.org/docs.html"},{"t":"Linux kernel: Pressure Stall Information","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html"},{"t":"KVM documentation","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Documents"}],"text":"A hypervisor host shares CPU, memory, disk and network among guests. Overcommit is what makes virtualization economical, and contention is its price. Operations means choosing ratios with evidence, watching the right signals and moving guests before users feel pain. What you will learn CPU, memory and storage overcommit and safe starting ratios Signals of contention: steal, ready time, ballooning, swapping and I/O wait NUMA, pinning and huge pages for demanding guests Placement and limits that contain noisy neighbours Overcommit by resource CPU can be overcommitted well, because most guests are idle most of the time. Common starting points are 4:1 virtual to physical cores for general workloads, lower (1:1 or 2:1) for latency-sensitive or heavy compute guests. Measure the real contention, not the ratio. Memory is risky to overcommit. When the host runs out, it swaps or kills guests. Use modest ratios (often 1.0 to 1.5), enable ballooning and page sharing only with monitoring, and keep a reserve for the host. Storage can be thin-provisioned, but the pool must be watched, since a full pool freezes many guests at once. Treat these as starting points to tune with data, because the right values depend on your workloads. Detect contention Inside the guest: high steal time ( %st in top or vmstat ) means the guest wanted CPU and the host did not give it. On the host: $ virsh nodecpustats $ virsh domstats --cpu-total --balloon --block --vcpu vm-web-01 $ virsh domblkstat vm-web-01 vda $ virsh domifstat vm-web-01 vnet3 $ numastat -m | head -20 $ cat /proc/pressure/cpu The pressure stall information in /proc/pressure/ shows how much time tasks wait for CPU, memory or I/O on the host. A host whose CPU pressure rises while guest steal rises is oversubscribed. Memory pressure plus swap use on the host is an emergency, because every guest suffers. NUMA, pinning and huge pages Large hosts have several NUMA nodes, each with local memory. A guest whose vCPUs run on one node but whose memory sits on another pays a latency cost. For performance-sensitive guests, keep vCPUs and memory on the same node (NUMA-aware placement), pin vCPUs to dedicated cores, and use huge pages to reduce page table overhead. These guests give up flexibility for predictability, so reserve them a dedicated host pool and do not overcommit it. Contain noisy neighbours Set limits and shares so a single guest cannot take everything: CPU shares and quotas, disk I/O throttling (IOPS and bandwidth) and network rate limits through libvirt or the cloud layer&#39;s flavors. Separate workload classes by host aggregate: a general pool with overcommit, a premium pool without it, a storage-heavy pool. Keep headroom to evacuate the largest host (N+1) and move guests early using live migration when a host trends toward contention. Predict first. A guest shows 25 percent steal while its own processes are idle. What does it mean? Your guess Answer: The hypervisor is not giving the guest the CPU it asks for: the host is oversubscribed. Move guests away, lower the ratio or reserve capacity. Steal is lost CPU time. Incident: The Monday morning stalls Incident Every Monday at 09:00 several unrelated application servers on the same host became slow for about 20 minutes. $ virsh domstats --cpu-total vm-batch-07 | grep cpu.time cpu.time=48230193847211 $ cat /proc/pressure/cpu some avg10=62.40 avg60=58.12 avg300=40.77 total=19238421831 A batch guest, scheduled to run weekly reports at 09:00, had 16 vCPUs and used all of them, while the host had 40 cores for 160 vCPUs of other guests. The other guests saw steal times above 20 percent. The team moved the batch guest to a dedicated aggregate with its own cores, set CPU shares, and added an alert on host CPU pressure and guest steal. Common mistakes Overcommitting memory like CPU Judging by the ratio instead of measured contention Mixing latency-sensitive and batch guests on one pool No spare capacity to evacuate a failed host"},"virtualization/virtualization-ops-libvirt-and-kvm-day-two-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>After the first guests are running, the work is routine and risky in equal parts: adding disks, changing memory, taking snapshots before changes, patching the host without downtime and keeping many hosts consistent. These tasks have safe, repeatable procedures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Inspect and adjust guests with \u003ccode>virsh\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Snapshots and backups, and their limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patching hosts with live migration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping host configuration consistent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-the-state\">Know the state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh list --all\nvirsh dominfo vm-web-01\nvirsh domblklist vm-web-01\nvirsh dumpxml vm-web-01 | head -40\nvirsh pool-list --details\nvirsh net-list --all\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh list --all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh dominfo vm-web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domblklist vm-web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh dumpxml vm-web-01 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh pool-list --details\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh net-list --all\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The domain XML is the source of truth for a guest: CPUs, memory, disks, NICs, devices. Treat it as configuration: keep copies, change it with \u003ccode>virsh edit\u003c/code> or an automation tool, and know that some changes apply only after a guest restart (use \u003ccode>--config\u003c/code> for persistent changes and \u003ccode>--live\u003c/code> for the running guest).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh setmem vm-web-01 4G --config --live\nvirsh attach-disk vm-web-01 /var/lib/libvirt/images/data2.qcow2 vdb --persistent --subdriver qcow2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh setmem vm-web-01 4G --config --live\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh attach-disk vm-web-01 /var/lib/libvirt/images/data2.qcow2 vdb --persistent --subdriver qcow2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots-and-backups\">Snapshots and backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A snapshot captures a guest&#39;s disk state (and optionally memory) at a moment. Internal snapshots live in the qcow2 file, external ones create an overlay file. Snapshots are good for short-term protection before a change, but long chains slow I/O and are fragile, and a snapshot on the same storage is not a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh snapshot-create-as vm-web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic --quiesce\nvirsh snapshot-list vm-web-01\nvirsh blockcommit vm-web-01 vda --active --pivot\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-create-as vm-web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic --quiesce\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list vm-web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh blockcommit vm-web-01 vda --active --pivot\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--quiesce\u003c/code> asks the guest agent to freeze the filesystem for a consistent image, which needs \u003ccode>qemu-guest-agent\u003c/code> in the guest. After the change is confirmed, merge the overlay back (blockcommit) and delete old snapshot metadata. For real backups, copy consistent images or use backup APIs to a separate system, and test restoring a guest on another host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"patching-a-host-without-downtime\">Patching a host without downtime\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Check capacity: the other hosts must have room for the guests on this one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mark the host unavailable for new guests (in a cloud layer, disable the compute service).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Live migrate\u003c/strong> guests away.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patch and reboot, check, and put the host back.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh migrate --live --persistent --undefinesource vm-web-01 qemu+ssh://kvm02/system\nvirsh domjobinfo vm-web-01\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh migrate --live --persistent --undefinesource vm-web-01 qemu+ssh://kvm02/system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domjobinfo vm-web-01\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Live migration needs compatible CPU models, shared or migratable storage, a stable network between hosts and enough bandwidth to copy dirty memory faster than the guest changes it. Guests with very high write rates may not converge; use auto-converge or post-copy options, or migrate in a quiet period.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consistent-hosts\">Consistent hosts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Manage hosts as code: the same kernel and QEMU versions per pool, the same network and storage definitions, one source for libvirt networks and pools. Drift between hosts is the root of failed migrations (&quot;works on kvm01, fails on kvm02&quot;).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A live migration to a patched host fails with a CPU feature error. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The destination host's CPU model or features differ from the source. Use a common CPU model for guests in a pool, or keep hosts of one generation together.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Migration needs compatible CPUs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-snapshot-chain\">Incident: The snapshot chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database guest became slower every week, and finally froze when its datastore filled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh snapshot-list db-prod-02 --tree | head\nqemu-img info --backing-chain /var/lib/libvirt/images/db-prod-02.pre-schema-change | grep -c &quot;image:&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list db-prod-02 --tree | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pre-upgrade-1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  +- pre-upgrade-2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">       |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">       +- pre-patch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">            |\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">            +- pre-schema-change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>qemu-img info --backing-chain /var/lib/libvirt/images/db-prod-02.pre-schema-change | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image:&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each change had added a snapshot &quot;just in case&quot; and nobody merged them. Reads had to walk nine layers, writes created large overlays, and the datastore ran out of space. The team merged the chain in a maintenance window, wrote a rule that snapshots live for 72 hours at most with an owner and a ticket, and added an alert on chain depth and datastore free space.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a snapshot on the same storage as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving snapshots for months\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing the running guest without \u003ccode>--config\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Live-migrating without checking CPU compatibility and capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"know-the-state","text":"Know the state"},{"id":"snapshots-and-backups","text":"Snapshots and backups"},{"id":"patching-a-host-without-downtime","text":"Patching a host without downtime"},{"id":"consistent-hosts","text":"Consistent hosts"},{"id":"incident-the-snapshot-chain","text":"Incident: The snapshot chain"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"virsh command reference","u":"https://libvirt.org/manpages/virsh.html"},{"t":"libvirt: migration","u":"https://libvirt.org/migration.html"},{"t":"QEMU documentation","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/"}],"text":"After the first guests are running, the work is routine and risky in equal parts: adding disks, changing memory, taking snapshots before changes, patching the host without downtime and keeping many hosts consistent. These tasks have safe, repeatable procedures. What you will learn Inspect and adjust guests with virsh Snapshots and backups, and their limits Patching hosts with live migration Keeping host configuration consistent Know the state $ virsh list --all $ virsh dominfo vm-web-01 $ virsh domblklist vm-web-01 $ virsh dumpxml vm-web-01 | head -40 $ virsh pool-list --details $ virsh net-list --all The domain XML is the source of truth for a guest: CPUs, memory, disks, NICs, devices. Treat it as configuration: keep copies, change it with virsh edit or an automation tool, and know that some changes apply only after a guest restart (use --config for persistent changes and --live for the running guest). $ virsh setmem vm-web-01 4G --config --live $ virsh attach-disk vm-web-01 /var/lib/libvirt/images/data2.qcow2 vdb --persistent --subdriver qcow2 Snapshots and backups A snapshot captures a guest&#39;s disk state (and optionally memory) at a moment. Internal snapshots live in the qcow2 file, external ones create an overlay file. Snapshots are good for short-term protection before a change, but long chains slow I/O and are fragile, and a snapshot on the same storage is not a backup. $ virsh snapshot-create-as vm-web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic --quiesce $ virsh snapshot-list vm-web-01 $ virsh blockcommit vm-web-01 vda --active --pivot --quiesce asks the guest agent to freeze the filesystem for a consistent image, which needs qemu-guest-agent in the guest. After the change is confirmed, merge the overlay back (blockcommit) and delete old snapshot metadata. For real backups, copy consistent images or use backup APIs to a separate system, and test restoring a guest on another host. Patching a host without downtime Check capacity: the other hosts must have room for the guests on this one. Mark the host unavailable for new guests (in a cloud layer, disable the compute service). Live migrate guests away. Patch and reboot, check, and put the host back. $ virsh migrate --live --persistent --undefinesource vm-web-01 qemu+ssh://kvm02/system $ virsh domjobinfo vm-web-01 Live migration needs compatible CPU models, shared or migratable storage, a stable network between hosts and enough bandwidth to copy dirty memory faster than the guest changes it. Guests with very high write rates may not converge; use auto-converge or post-copy options, or migrate in a quiet period. Consistent hosts Manage hosts as code: the same kernel and QEMU versions per pool, the same network and storage definitions, one source for libvirt networks and pools. Drift between hosts is the root of failed migrations (\"works on kvm01, fails on kvm02\"). Predict first. A live migration to a patched host fails with a CPU feature error. What is the likely cause? Your guess Answer: The destination host's CPU model or features differ from the source. Use a common CPU model for guests in a pool, or keep hosts of one generation together. Migration needs compatible CPUs. Incident: The snapshot chain Incident A database guest became slower every week, and finally froze when its datastore filled. $ virsh snapshot-list db-prod-02 --tree | head pre-upgrade-1 | +- pre-upgrade-2 | +- pre-patch | +- pre-schema-change $ qemu-img info --backing-chain /var/lib/libvirt/images/db-prod-02.pre-schema-change | grep -c \"image:\" 9 Each change had added a snapshot \"just in case\" and nobody merged them. Reads had to walk nine layers, writes created large overlays, and the datastore ran out of space. The team merged the chain in a maintenance window, wrote a rule that snapshots live for 72 hours at most with an owner and a ticket, and added an alert on chain depth and datastore free space. Common mistakes Treating a snapshot on the same storage as a backup Leaving snapshots for months Changing "},"virtualization/virtualization-ops-guest-performance-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>&quot;The VM is slow&quot; can mean the application, the guest operating system, the hypervisor, shared storage or the network. The fastest way to find the layer is to compare two views of the same moment: what the guest sees and what the host sees.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A layered method for guest slowness\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paired guest and host checks for CPU, memory, disk and network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Virtio drivers, guest agents and common misconfigurations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to move, resize or fix the guest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"method-top-down-then-bottom-up\">Method: top-down, then bottom-up\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Define the symptom\u003c/strong> and the time window. Does it affect one guest, many guests on one host, or many hosts?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Inside the guest\u003c/strong>: check the USE signals (CPU, memory, disk, network), the application and the kernel log.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>On the host\u003c/strong>: check the same resources for this guest and for the host overall.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Shared layers\u003c/strong>: storage latency, network paths and the management plane.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>If many guests on one host slow down together, suspect the host or its storage. If only one guest slows down, look at its own configuration or load first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cpu-and-memory-pairs\">CPU and memory pairs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Guest signal\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Host check\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>High \u003ccode>%steal\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Host CPU pressure, vCPU to core ratio\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>High load, low CPU use\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Guest I/O wait; host disk latency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Memory pressure, swapping\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Balloon size, host free memory\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Single vCPU saturated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Guest app is single-threaded\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh domstats --balloon --cpu-total vm-api-03\nvirsh vcpuinfo vm-api-03\nvirt-top -n 1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domstats --balloon --cpu-total vm-api-03\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh vcpuinfo vm-api-03\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virt-top -n 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the balloon driver has taken memory from the guest (\u003ccode>balloon.current\u003c/code> below \u003ccode>balloon.maximum\u003c/code>) while the guest swaps, the host is short on memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disk-and-network-pairs\">Disk and network pairs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -xz 1 3\nvirsh domblkstat vm-api-03 vda --human\nvirsh domiflist vm-api-03\nvirsh domifstat vm-api-03 vnet5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>iostat -xz 1 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domblkstat vm-api-03 vda --human\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domiflist vm-api-03\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh domifstat vm-api-03 vnet5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Compare the guest&#39;s \u003ccode>await\u003c/code> with the latency at the storage layer. A large gap points to the hypervisor&#39;s I/O path (queueing, throttles); both high points at storage. For network, a growing \u003ccode>drop\u003c/code> count on the host&#39;s \u003ccode>vnet\u003c/code> interface while the guest sends normally means the host side is dropping, perhaps because of a saturated bridge, a full qdisc or a throttle. Use \u003cstrong>virtio\u003c/strong> drivers (\u003ccode>virtio-blk\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>virtio-scsi\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>virtio-net\u003c/code>) with multiqueue for busy guests; emulated devices such as IDE or e1000 are much slower, and are a classic silent cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configuration-checks\">Configuration checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check the XML for a sensible CPU model (host-passthrough or a modern model for performance), \u003ccode>cache\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>io\u003c/code> modes on disks, the machine type, whether the guest agent is connected, and clock settings. Time drift in guests can break certificates and clustering, so use \u003ccode>chrony\u003c/code> with the KVM clock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Many guests on one host show high disk await at the same time, but another host's guests are fine. Where do you look first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> At that host and its storage path: the host's disks or multipath links, the shared storage backend and a noisy guest generating I/O on the host.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Common symptoms across guests point below them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-slow-network-inside-the-hypervisor\">Incident: The slow network inside the hypervisor\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>File transfers between two guests on the same host topped out at a small fraction of what the 10 Gb/s network should allow.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh dumpxml vm-files-01 | grep -A3 &quot;&lt;interface&quot;\nethtool -S vnet4 | grep -i drop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh dumpxml vm-files-01 | grep -A3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&lt;interface&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;interface type='bridge'&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &lt;model type='e1000'/&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ethtool -S vnet4 | grep -i drop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     rx_dropped: 183921\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The guest used the emulated \u003ccode>e1000\u003c/code> network card, which consumes a lot of CPU in the hypervisor, and a single-queue configuration. After changing the model to \u003ccode>virtio\u003c/code> with multiqueue and installing the paravirtual driver in the guest, throughput grew several times and the drops disappeared. The team made virtio the default in their guest templates and added a check for emulated devices to their audit scripts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Diagnosing only inside the guest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving emulated devices on busy guests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring balloon and host memory pressure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resizing a guest before locating the bottleneck\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"method-top-down-then-bottom-up","text":"Method: top-down, then bottom-up"},{"id":"cpu-and-memory-pairs","text":"CPU and memory pairs"},{"id":"disk-and-network-pairs","text":"Disk and network pairs"},{"id":"configuration-checks","text":"Configuration checks"},{"id":"incident-the-slow-network-inside-the-hypervisor","text":"Incident: The slow network inside the hypervisor"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt: domain XML format","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatdomain.html"},{"t":"KVM: Virtio","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Virtio"},{"t":"Brendan Gregg: The USE Method for Linux","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/USEmethod/use-linux.html"}],"text":"\"The VM is slow\" can mean the application, the guest operating system, the hypervisor, shared storage or the network. The fastest way to find the layer is to compare two views of the same moment: what the guest sees and what the host sees. What you will learn A layered method for guest slowness Paired guest and host checks for CPU, memory, disk and network Virtio drivers, guest agents and common misconfigurations When to move, resize or fix the guest Method: top-down, then bottom-up Define the symptom and the time window. Does it affect one guest, many guests on one host, or many hosts? Inside the guest : check the USE signals (CPU, memory, disk, network), the application and the kernel log. On the host : check the same resources for this guest and for the host overall. Shared layers : storage latency, network paths and the management plane. If many guests on one host slow down together, suspect the host or its storage. If only one guest slows down, look at its own configuration or load first. CPU and memory pairs Guest signal Host check High %steal Host CPU pressure, vCPU to core ratio High load, low CPU use Guest I/O wait; host disk latency Memory pressure, swapping Balloon size, host free memory Single vCPU saturated Guest app is single-threaded $ virsh domstats --balloon --cpu-total vm-api-03 $ virsh vcpuinfo vm-api-03 $ virt-top -n 1 If the balloon driver has taken memory from the guest ( balloon.current below balloon.maximum ) while the guest swaps, the host is short on memory. Disk and network pairs $ iostat -xz 1 3 $ virsh domblkstat vm-api-03 vda --human $ virsh domiflist vm-api-03 $ virsh domifstat vm-api-03 vnet5 Compare the guest&#39;s await with the latency at the storage layer. A large gap points to the hypervisor&#39;s I/O path (queueing, throttles); both high points at storage. For network, a growing drop count on the host&#39;s vnet interface while the guest sends normally means the host side is dropping, perhaps because of a saturated bridge, a full qdisc or a throttle. Use virtio drivers ( virtio-blk or virtio-scsi , virtio-net ) with multiqueue for busy guests; emulated devices such as IDE or e1000 are much slower, and are a classic silent cause. Configuration checks Check the XML for a sensible CPU model (host-passthrough or a modern model for performance), cache and io modes on disks, the machine type, whether the guest agent is connected, and clock settings. Time drift in guests can break certificates and clustering, so use chrony with the KVM clock. Predict first. Many guests on one host show high disk await at the same time, but another host's guests are fine. Where do you look first? Your guess Answer: At that host and its storage path: the host's disks or multipath links, the shared storage backend and a noisy guest generating I/O on the host. Common symptoms across guests point below them. Incident: The slow network inside the hypervisor Incident File transfers between two guests on the same host topped out at a small fraction of what the 10 Gb/s network should allow. $ virsh dumpxml vm-files-01 | grep -A3 \"\u003cinterface\" \u003cinterface type='bridge'> \u003cmodel type='e1000'/> $ ethtool -S vnet4 | grep -i drop rx_dropped: 183921 The guest used the emulated e1000 network card, which consumes a lot of CPU in the hypervisor, and a single-queue configuration. After changing the model to virtio with multiqueue and installing the paravirtual driver in the guest, throughput grew several times and the drops disappeared. The team made virtio the default in their guest templates and added a check for emulated devices to their audit scripts. Common mistakes Diagnosing only inside the guest Leaving emulated devices on busy guests Ignoring balloon and host memory pressure Resizing a guest before locating the bottleneck"},"virtualization/vm-backup-replication-and-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Virtual machines make copying easy and recovery tempting to take for granted. The details of consistency, storage location and restore speed decide whether a backup actually saves you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Snapshots versus backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Application-consistent backups and guest agents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication and failover to a second site\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore testing and retention\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots-and-backups\">Snapshots and backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>snapshot\u003c/strong> records a VM&#39;s disk state at a moment and stores changes separately. It lives on the same storage and slows down as it grows, so use it for short-lived rollback points before a change. A \u003cstrong>backup\u003c/strong> is a copy on separate storage with retention, and it survives loss of the primary storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic\nvirsh snapshot-list web-01\nvirsh blockcommit web-01 vda --active --pivot     # merge the snapshot back when done\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic\nvirsh snapshot-list web-01\nvirsh blockcommit web-01 vda --active --pivot     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># merge the snapshot back when done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consistency\">Consistency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A crash-consistent copy is like pulling the power: filesystems usually recover, but databases may need repair. For \u003cstrong>application-consistent\u003c/strong> backups, use the guest agent to freeze the filesystem or tell the application to flush data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh domfsfreeze web-01\n# take the storage snapshot or copy\nvirsh domfsthaw web-01\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">virsh domfsfreeze web-01\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># take the storage snapshot or copy\u003c/span>\nvirsh domfsthaw web-01\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep the freeze short, and use database-native backups for large critical databases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication\">Replication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Replicate changed blocks to another site on a schedule or continuously, so that a failover boots a recent copy. Know your \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong>, set by replication lag, and \u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong>, set by how fast the copy can boot and the network can be reconfigured.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore-tests\">Restore tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Schedule restores into an isolated network, boot the VM, check that the service answers and record the time. Keep retention tiers such as daily, weekly and monthly, and protect some copies from deletion, for example with immutable storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-snapshot-that-filled-the-datastore\">Incident: The snapshot that filled the datastore\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A long-lived snapshot grew until it filled storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">datastore: 100% used\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-01 snapshot delta: 1.2 TB, snapshot age 71 days\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">VMs paused: 38\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had used a snapshot as a backup before an upgrade and forgot to remove it. The delta grew until the datastore filled and VMs paused. The team limited snapshot age by policy, added alerts and moved to scheduled backups on separate storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using snapshots as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups on the same storage as the VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring application consistency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"snapshots-and-backups","text":"Snapshots and backups"},{"id":"consistency","text":"Consistency"},{"id":"replication","text":"Replication"},{"id":"restore-tests","text":"Restore tests"},{"id":"incident-the-snapshot-that-filled-the-datastore","text":"Incident: The snapshot that filled the datastore"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt documentation","u":"https://libvirt.org/docs.html"},{"t":"Proxmox documentation","u":"https://pve.proxmox.com/pve-docs/"}],"text":"Virtual machines make copying easy and recovery tempting to take for granted. The details of consistency, storage location and restore speed decide whether a backup actually saves you. What you will learn Snapshots versus backups Application-consistent backups and guest agents Replication and failover to a second site Restore testing and retention Snapshots and backups A snapshot records a VM&#39;s disk state at a moment and stores changes separately. It lives on the same storage and slows down as it grows, so use it for short-lived rollback points before a change. A backup is a copy on separate storage with retention, and it survives loss of the primary storage. virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 pre-upgrade --disk-only --atomic virsh snapshot-list web-01 virsh blockcommit web-01 vda --active --pivot # merge the snapshot back when done Consistency A crash-consistent copy is like pulling the power: filesystems usually recover, but databases may need repair. For application-consistent backups, use the guest agent to freeze the filesystem or tell the application to flush data. virsh domfsfreeze web-01 # take the storage snapshot or copy virsh domfsthaw web-01 Keep the freeze short, and use database-native backups for large critical databases. Replication Replicate changed blocks to another site on a schedule or continuously, so that a failover boots a recent copy. Know your RPO , set by replication lag, and RTO , set by how fast the copy can boot and the network can be reconfigured. Restore tests Schedule restores into an isolated network, boot the VM, check that the service answers and record the time. Keep retention tiers such as daily, weekly and monthly, and protect some copies from deletion, for example with immutable storage. Incident: The snapshot that filled the datastore Incident A long-lived snapshot grew until it filled storage. datastore: 100% used web-01 snapshot delta: 1.2 TB, snapshot age 71 days VMs paused: 38 Someone had used a snapshot as a backup before an upgrade and forgot to remove it. The delta grew until the datastore filled and VMs paused. The team limited snapshot age by policy, added alerts and moved to scheduled backups on separate storage. Common mistakes Using snapshots as backups Backups on the same storage as the VMs Never testing a restore Ignoring application consistency"},"virtualization/vm-templates-cloud-init-and-image-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>Every VM starts from an image. If the image is a mystery, every VM is a mystery. Treat images like software: built from source, tested and versioned.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why hand-built templates drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building images with Packer and configuration management\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning, hardening and scanning images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing, versioning and retiring images\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-pipeline\">A pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"source &quot;qemu&quot; &quot;ubuntu&quot; {\n  iso_url          = &quot;https://releases.ubuntu.com/24.04/ubuntu-24.04-live-server-amd64.iso&quot;\n  iso_checksum     = &quot;sha256:REPLACE_WITH_CHECKSUM&quot;\n  output_directory = &quot;output&quot;\n  disk_size        = &quot;20G&quot;\n  ssh_username     = &quot;builder&quot;\n  ssh_private_key_file = &quot;~/.ssh/builder&quot;\n}\n\nbuild {\n  sources = [&quot;source.qemu.ubuntu&quot;]\n  provisioner &quot;ansible&quot; {\n    playbook_file = &quot;playbooks/base-image.yml&quot;\n  }\n  provisioner &quot;shell&quot; {\n    script = &quot;scripts/cleanup.sh&quot;\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">source \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;qemu&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ubuntu&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  iso_url\u003c/span>          = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://releases.ubuntu.com/24.04/ubuntu-24.04-live-server-amd64.iso&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  iso_checksum\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sha256:REPLACE_WITH_CHECKSUM&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  output_directory\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;output&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  disk_size\u003c/span>        = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;20G&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  ssh_username\u003c/span>     = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;builder&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  ssh_private_key_file\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;~/.ssh/builder&quot;\u003c/span>\n}\n\nbuild {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  sources\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;source.qemu.ubuntu&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  provisioner \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ansible&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    playbook_file\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;playbooks/base-image.yml&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n  provisioner \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shell&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">    script\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;scripts/cleanup.sh&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"packer init . &amp;&amp; packer validate . &amp;&amp; packer build .\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">packer init . &amp;&amp; packer validate . &amp;&amp; packer build .\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pin the ISO checksum and run the build in CI with a clean builder. Use the same configuration management tool you use on servers, so images and running hosts follow one standard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clean-and-harden\">Clean and harden\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cloud-init clean --logs --seed\ntruncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id\nrm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* /root/.bash_history\napt-get clean &amp;&amp; fstrim -av\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">cloud-init clean --logs --seed\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">truncate\u003c/span> -s 0 /etc/machine-id\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* /root/.bash_history\napt-get clean &amp;&amp; fstrim -av\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Remove machine IDs, SSH host keys, logs and history so clones do not share identity. Apply a security baseline, disable unused services and scan the image for vulnerabilities and misconfiguration. Do not include secrets or personal SSH keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-at-boot\">Identity at boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>cloud-init\u003c/strong> to set hostname, users, keys, network and join configuration on first boot, so one image serves every role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publish-and-retire\">Publish and retire\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test each image by booting it, checking that cloud-init completes and running a few smoke tests. Name images with a date or version, record the build commit and package list and keep only a few recent versions. Set a maximum age and rebuild monthly or when critical patches land.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-hundred-clones-with-the-same-host-keys\">Incident: Two hundred clones with the same host keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cloned VMs shared SSH host keys because the template was not cleaned.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-01 host key SHA256:q1xA...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-02 host key SHA256:q1xA...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">identical host key on 212 hosts\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The template had been converted without removing SSH host keys, so every clone kept them. Clients could not tell the hosts apart, and one compromised key mattered everywhere. The pipeline now cleans identity data, and a test checks that two instances never share a host key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Templates edited by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving machine-specific data in images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rebuilding for patches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No smoke test for new images\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-pipeline","text":"A pipeline"},{"id":"clean-and-harden","text":"Clean and harden"},{"id":"identity-at-boot","text":"Identity at boot"},{"id":"publish-and-retire","text":"Publish and retire"},{"id":"incident-two-hundred-clones-with-the-same-host-keys","text":"Incident: Two hundred clones with the same host keys"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"HashiCorp Packer documentation","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/packer/docs"},{"t":"cloud-init documentation","u":"https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"}],"text":"Every VM starts from an image. If the image is a mystery, every VM is a mystery. Treat images like software: built from source, tested and versioned. What you will learn Why hand-built templates drift Building images with Packer and configuration management Cleaning, hardening and scanning images Publishing, versioning and retiring images A pipeline source \"qemu\" \"ubuntu\" { iso_url = \"https://releases.ubuntu.com/24.04/ubuntu-24.04-live-server-amd64.iso\" iso_checksum = \"sha256:REPLACE_WITH_CHECKSUM\" output_directory = \"output\" disk_size = \"20G\" ssh_username = \"builder\" ssh_private_key_file = \"~/.ssh/builder\" } build { sources = [ \"source.qemu.ubuntu\" ] provisioner \"ansible\" { playbook_file = \"playbooks/base-image.yml\" } provisioner \"shell\" { script = \"scripts/cleanup.sh\" } } packer init . && packer validate . && packer build . Pin the ISO checksum and run the build in CI with a clean builder. Use the same configuration management tool you use on servers, so images and running hosts follow one standard. Clean and harden cloud-init clean --logs --seed truncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_* /root/.bash_history apt-get clean && fstrim -av Remove machine IDs, SSH host keys, logs and history so clones do not share identity. Apply a security baseline, disable unused services and scan the image for vulnerabilities and misconfiguration. Do not include secrets or personal SSH keys. Identity at boot Use cloud-init to set hostname, users, keys, network and join configuration on first boot, so one image serves every role. Publish and retire Test each image by booting it, checking that cloud-init completes and running a few smoke tests. Name images with a date or version, record the build commit and package list and keep only a few recent versions. Set a maximum age and rebuild monthly or when critical patches land. Incident: Two hundred clones with the same host keys Incident Cloned VMs shared SSH host keys because the template was not cleaned. web-01 host key SHA256:q1xA... web-02 host key SHA256:q1xA... identical host key on 212 hosts The template had been converted without removing SSH host keys, so every clone kept them. Clients could not tell the hosts apart, and one compromised key mattered everywhere. The pipeline now cleans identity data, and a test checks that two instances never share a host key. Common mistakes Templates edited by hand Leaving machine-specific data in images Never rebuilding for patches No smoke test for new images"},"virtualization/gpu-passthrough-and-device-assignment":{"html":"\u003cp>Some workloads need real hardware speed, such as GPU compute or high-rate networking. Passthrough gives a VM direct control of a device, at the cost of the usual virtualization conveniences.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>IOMMU, VFIO and IOMMU groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assigning a device to a VM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limits: migration, reset and sharing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security considerations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>IOMMU\u003c/strong> translates and isolates device memory access. \u003cstrong>VFIO\u003c/strong> is the kernel framework that lets user space, such as QEMU, own a device safely. Devices that share an \u003cstrong>IOMMU group\u003c/strong> must be assigned together, because the hardware cannot isolate them from each other.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# enable in kernel command line: intel_iommu=on iommu=pt  (or amd_iommu=on)\ndmesg | grep -i -E &quot;iommu|dmar&quot;\nfor g in /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*; do echo &quot;group ${g##*/}:&quot;; ls &quot;$g/devices&quot;; done\nlspci -nnk -s 3b:00.0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># enable in kernel command line: intel_iommu=on iommu=pt  (or amd_iommu=on)\u003c/span>\ndmesg | grep -i -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;iommu|dmar&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> g \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;group \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${g##*/}\u003c/span>:&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$g\u003c/span>/devices&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\nlspci -nnk -s 3b:00.0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"assign-a-device\">Assign a device\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo &quot;options vfio-pci ids=10de:2330&quot; &gt; /etc/modprobe.d/vfio.conf\nupdate-initramfs -u &amp;&amp; reboot\nvirsh nodedev-list --cap pci | grep 3b\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;options vfio-pci ids=10de:2330&quot;\u003c/span> &gt; /etc/modprobe.d/vfio.conf\nupdate-initramfs -u &amp;&amp; reboot\nvirsh nodedev-list --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cap\u003c/span> pci | grep 3b\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"xml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"&lt;hostdev mode='subsystem' type='pci' managed='yes'&gt;\n  &lt;source&gt;\n    &lt;address domain='0x0000' bus='0x3b' slot='0x00' function='0x0'/&gt;\n  &lt;/source&gt;\n&lt;/hostdev&gt;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-name\">hostdev\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;subsystem&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;pci&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">managed\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;yes&#x27;\u003c/span>&gt;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-name\">source\u003c/span>&gt;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-name\">address\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">domain\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0x0000&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bus\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0x3b&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">slot\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0x00&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">function\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0x0&#x27;\u003c/span>/&gt;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-name\">source\u003c/span>&gt;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-name\">hostdev\u003c/span>&gt;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>managed=&#39;yes&#39;\u003c/code>, libvirt binds the device to VFIO when the VM starts and returns it afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A passed-through device belongs to one VM at a time. \u003cstrong>Live migration\u003c/strong> is generally not possible with it attached. Reset behavior varies by device, so test repeated start and stop cycles. Large device memory needs matching guest settings, such as huge pages and a large BAR configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security\">Security\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assignment relies on the IOMMU for isolation. Keep firmware and drivers updated, restrict who can attach devices and prefer hardware partitioning, such as MIG or SR-IOV, when several tenants share a device.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh dumpxml gpu-vm | grep -A3 hostdev\nnvidia-smi -L        # run inside the guest\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">virsh dumpxml gpu-vm | grep -A3 hostdev\nnvidia-smi -L        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># run inside the guest\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-host-that-froze-on-shutdown\">Incident: The host that froze on shutdown\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A VM shutdown hung the whole host because the GPU could not reset.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vfio-pci 0000:3b:00.0: not ready 65535ms after FLR; giving up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">qemu-kvm: terminating on signal 15\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host unresponsive for 4 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The device did not support a clean function-level reset in that firmware version. The team updated the firmware, chose a different reset method, added a stop-and-start test to acceptance and wrote a runbook for recovering a stuck device.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Not checking IOMMU groups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting live migration with passthrough devices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping reset tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing a passthrough device between tenants\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works"},{"id":"assign-a-device","text":"Assign a device"},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits"},{"id":"security","text":"Security"},{"id":"incident-the-host-that-froze-on-shutdown","text":"Incident: The host that froze on shutdown"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt PCI passthrough","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatdomain.html#pci-devices"},{"t":"Linux VFIO documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/vfio.html"}],"text":"Some workloads need real hardware speed, such as GPU compute or high-rate networking. Passthrough gives a VM direct control of a device, at the cost of the usual virtualization conveniences. What you will learn IOMMU, VFIO and IOMMU groups Assigning a device to a VM Limits: migration, reset and sharing Security considerations How it works The IOMMU translates and isolates device memory access. VFIO is the kernel framework that lets user space, such as QEMU, own a device safely. Devices that share an IOMMU group must be assigned together, because the hardware cannot isolate them from each other. # enable in kernel command line: intel_iommu=on iommu=pt (or amd_iommu=on) dmesg | grep -i -E \"iommu|dmar\" for g in /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/*; do echo \"group ${g##*/} :\" ; ls \" $g /devices\" ; done lspci -nnk -s 3b:00.0 Assign a device echo \"options vfio-pci ids=10de:2330\" > /etc/modprobe.d/vfio.conf update-initramfs -u && reboot virsh nodedev-list -- cap pci | grep 3b \u003c hostdev mode = &#x27;subsystem&#x27; type = &#x27;pci&#x27; managed = &#x27;yes&#x27; > \u003c source > \u003c address domain = &#x27;0x0000&#x27; bus = &#x27;0x3b&#x27; slot = &#x27;0x00&#x27; function = &#x27;0x0&#x27; /> \u003c/ source > \u003c/ hostdev > With managed=&#39;yes&#39; , libvirt binds the device to VFIO when the VM starts and returns it afterwards. Limits A passed-through device belongs to one VM at a time. Live migration is generally not possible with it attached. Reset behavior varies by device, so test repeated start and stop cycles. Large device memory needs matching guest settings, such as huge pages and a large BAR configuration. Security Assignment relies on the IOMMU for isolation. Keep firmware and drivers updated, restrict who can attach devices and prefer hardware partitioning, such as MIG or SR-IOV, when several tenants share a device. virsh dumpxml gpu-vm | grep -A3 hostdev nvidia-smi -L # run inside the guest Incident: The host that froze on shutdown Incident A VM shutdown hung the whole host because the GPU could not reset. vfio-pci 0000:3b:00.0: not ready 65535ms after FLR; giving up qemu-kvm: terminating on signal 15 host unresponsive for 4 minutes The device did not support a clean function-level reset in that firmware version. The team updated the firmware, chose a different reset method, added a stop-and-start test to acceptance and wrote a runbook for recovering a stuck device. Common mistakes Not checking IOMMU groups Expecting live migration with passthrough devices Skipping reset tests Sharing a passthrough device between tenants"},"virtualization/hypervisor-security-and-isolation":{"html":"\u003cp>The hypervisor is the most privileged software in the stack. If a guest can reach or break it, the boundary between tenants disappears, so hardening it is part of daily operations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reducing the attack surface of the host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolating management, storage and guest networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confinement and device emulation risks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patching, mitigations and trust separation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"minimal-host\">Minimal host\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Install only what the hypervisor needs. Disable unused services and emulated devices in guests, such as floppy or legacy USB controllers, since device emulation code has historically contained escape bugs. Keep the host operating system, QEMU and firmware patched on a defined schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-separation\">Network separation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"management network   : hypervisor admin, BMC       (jump host only)\nstorage network      : NFS, iSCSI, Ceph traffic     (hypervisors and storage only)\nmigration network    : live migration traffic       (hypervisors only)\nguest networks       : VLANs per tenant or tier     (no route to the above)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">management network   : hypervisor admin, BMC       (jump host only)\nstorage network      : NFS, iSCSI, Ceph traffic     (hypervisors and storage only)\nmigration network    : live migration traffic       (hypervisors only)\nguest networks       : VLANs per tenant or tier     (no route to the above)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Guests must never reach management or storage networks. Enforce this with VLANs, firewall rules and no routes. Protect live migration traffic with encryption if it crosses shared links.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"confinement\">Confinement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ps -eZ | grep qemu                      # SELinux labels per VM (sVirt)\nvirsh dumpxml web-01 | grep -A2 seclabel\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ps -eZ | grep qemu                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># SELinux labels per VM (sVirt)\u003c/span>\nvirsh dumpxml web-01 | grep -A2 seclabel\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>sVirt\u003c/strong> or AppArmor profiles give each VM process its own label, so a compromised QEMU process cannot read other VMs&#39; disks. Run QEMU as an unprivileged user.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-and-data\">Access and data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restrict who can run \u003ccode>virsh\u003c/code> or use the management console, require strong authentication and log every action. Treat disk images and snapshots as sensitive data, because anyone who can copy an image has the machine&#39;s data. Encrypt storage where required.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mitigations-and-trust\">Mitigations and trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Follow vendor guidance on CPU side-channel mitigations and understand their performance cost. Do not run high-risk, untrusted workloads on the same hosts as sensitive ones. Use separate clusters or dedicated hosts for different trust levels.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-management-port-in-the-guest-vlan\">Incident: The management port in the guest VLAN\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A guest could reach the hypervisor management interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nmap -p 16509,22 10.60.0.1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nmap -p 16509,22 10.60.0.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">22/tcp    open  ssh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">16509/tcp open  libvirt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">route to management network present\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A flat network let guests see the hypervisor&#39;s management addresses. Nothing had been exploited, but the exposure was real. The team put management on a separate VLAN, removed routes from guest networks and added a recurring scan from a test guest that must fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Management interfaces reachable from guests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running QEMU as root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpatched hypervisors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing untrusted and sensitive workloads on one host\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"minimal-host","text":"Minimal host"},{"id":"network-separation","text":"Network separation"},{"id":"confinement","text":"Confinement"},{"id":"access-and-data","text":"Access and data"},{"id":"mitigations-and-trust","text":"Mitigations and trust"},{"id":"incident-the-management-port-in-the-guest-vlan","text":"Incident: The management port in the guest VLAN"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt security","u":"https://libvirt.org/securityprocess.html"},{"t":"QEMU security","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/security.html"}],"text":"The hypervisor is the most privileged software in the stack. If a guest can reach or break it, the boundary between tenants disappears, so hardening it is part of daily operations. What you will learn Reducing the attack surface of the host Isolating management, storage and guest networks Confinement and device emulation risks Patching, mitigations and trust separation Minimal host Install only what the hypervisor needs. Disable unused services and emulated devices in guests, such as floppy or legacy USB controllers, since device emulation code has historically contained escape bugs. Keep the host operating system, QEMU and firmware patched on a defined schedule. Network separation management network : hypervisor admin, BMC (jump host only) storage network : NFS, iSCSI, Ceph traffic (hypervisors and storage only) migration network : live migration traffic (hypervisors only) guest networks : VLANs per tenant or tier (no route to the above) Guests must never reach management or storage networks. Enforce this with VLANs, firewall rules and no routes. Protect live migration traffic with encryption if it crosses shared links. Confinement ps -eZ | grep qemu # SELinux labels per VM (sVirt) virsh dumpxml web-01 | grep -A2 seclabel sVirt or AppArmor profiles give each VM process its own label, so a compromised QEMU process cannot read other VMs&#39; disks. Run QEMU as an unprivileged user. Access and data Restrict who can run virsh or use the management console, require strong authentication and log every action. Treat disk images and snapshots as sensitive data, because anyone who can copy an image has the machine&#39;s data. Encrypt storage where required. Mitigations and trust Follow vendor guidance on CPU side-channel mitigations and understand their performance cost. Do not run high-risk, untrusted workloads on the same hosts as sensitive ones. Use separate clusters or dedicated hosts for different trust levels. Incident: The management port in the guest VLAN Incident A guest could reach the hypervisor management interface. $ nmap -p 16509,22 10.60.0.1 22/tcp open ssh 16509/tcp open libvirt route to management network present A flat network let guests see the hypervisor&#39;s management addresses. Nothing had been exploited, but the exposure was real. The team put management on a separate VLAN, removed routes from guest networks and added a recurring scan from a test guest that must fail. Common mistakes Management interfaces reachable from guests Running QEMU as root Unpatched hypervisors Mixing untrusted and sensitive workloads on one host"}});